**Definition → Why we use it → Real life example → System design example**

---

# 1. Functional Requirements

### Definition

Functional requirements describe **what the system should do**.

They define the actual features and behavior expected from the system.

### Why do we use them?

Before designing a system, we need to know what functionality it must provide.

### Real life example

For a food delivery application:

• User can search restaurants
• User can order food
• Restaurant can accept orders
• Delivery partner can receive orders
• User can make payment
• User can track delivery

These are functional requirements.

### System design example

For YouTube:

• Upload video
• Watch video
• Search video
• Like video
• Comment
• Subscribe to channel

---

# 2. Non Functional Requirements

### Definition

Non functional requirements describe **how well the system should work**.

They are related to qualities such as speed, availability, scalability, security and reliability.

### Why do we use them?

Two systems can provide the same functionality but have completely different quality.

For example:

Application A takes 10 seconds to load.

Application B takes 1 second.

Both provide the same functionality, but B has better performance.

### Real life example

For a banking application:

• Response should be fast
• System should be available almost all the time
• Transactions should be reliable
• User data should be secure
• System should handle millions of users

---

# 3. CDN

**Content Delivery Network**

### Definition

A CDN is a network of servers distributed across different geographical locations that delivers content from a server closer to the user.

### Why do we use it?

To reduce:

• Latency
• Server load
• Network distance
• Loading time

### Real life example

Suppose you are in India and watch a video hosted on a server in the USA.

Without CDN:

User → USA Server → User

With CDN:

User → India CDN Server → User

The content reaches you faster.

### System design example

For Netflix:

Movies and images can be cached on CDN servers around the world.

User in India gets content from a nearby CDN location.

