# 7. Messaging Queue

### Definition

A messaging queue temporarily stores messages between producers and consumers.

### Why do we use it?

It allows systems to process tasks **asynchronously**.

It also prevents one slow service from blocking another service.

### Real life example

Imagine a restaurant.

Customer gives order.

The waiter does not personally cook the food.

Instead:

```text
Customer
   ↓
Order
   ↓
Kitchen Queue
   ↓
Chef
```

The order waits in the queue until the chef processes it.

### Technology examples

Kafka

RabbitMQ

Amazon SQS

### System example

When a user uploads a video:

```text
User
 ↓
Video Server
 ↓
Message Queue
 ↓
Video Processing Service
```

The user does not need to wait while the video is processed.

---

# 8. Connection Pooling

### Definition

Connection pooling means maintaining a collection of reusable database connections.

### Why do we use it?

Creating a new database connection every time is expensive.

Instead:

```text
Application
 ↓
Connection Pool
 ↓
Database
```

The application reuses existing connections.

### Real life example

Imagine a taxi company.

Instead of buying a new taxi every time someone needs a ride, the company keeps a fleet of taxis and reuses them.

### Without connection pooling

```text
Request
 ↓
Create connection
 ↓
Database
 ↓
Close connection
```

Repeated thousands of times.

### With pooling

```text
Connection Pool
 ↓ ↓ ↓ ↓
Database
```

Connections are reused.

