# 11. Caching

### Definition

Caching stores frequently accessed data temporarily so it can be retrieved faster.

### Why do we use it?

It reduces:

• Database load
• Response time
• Network traffic

### Real life example

Imagine you frequently use a book.

Instead of going to the library every time, you keep the book on your desk.

That is similar to caching.

### System example

Without cache:

```text
User
 ↓
Application
 ↓
Database
```

With cache:

```text
User
 ↓
Application
 ↓
Cache
 ↓
Database only when needed
```

### Popular technology

Redis

---

# 12. Idempotency

### Definition

An operation is idempotent when performing it multiple times produces the same final result as performing it once.

### Why do we use it?

It prevents duplicate operations when a request is accidentally sent multiple times.

### Real life example

Suppose you click **Pay ₹500**.

Network fails.

Your application sends the payment request again.

Without idempotency:

```text
₹500 + ₹500 = ₹1000 deducted
```

With idempotency:

```text
First request → Payment successful
Second request → Already processed
```

### Common use

Payment systems

Order creation

Booking systems

Bank transactions

---

# 13. Single Point of Failure

### Definition

A single point of failure is one component whose failure can bring down the entire system.

### Why do we avoid it?

Because one failure should not destroy the entire application.

### Bad architecture

```text
Users
  ↓
One Server
  ↓
Database
```

If the server crashes:

```text
Entire application DOWN
```

### Better architecture

```text
        Load Balancer
        /           \
   Server 1       Server 2
```

If Server 1 fails, Server 2 can continue serving users.

